course: biology
lesson: 233
title: Animal minds: clever crows, ravens, jays and parrots
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «Bird brain» is used as an insult, but crows, ravens, jays and parrots are some of the cleverest animals on Earth.
explain: Their brains are small, but the nerve cells are packed very tightly, so a crow's walnut-sized brain holds about as many as some monkey brains.
explain: Watch a crow at a car park: it may drop a hard nut where cars drive over it, wait, and then hop out to eat the cracked nut.
explain: Clever birds make tools, plan ahead, remember thousands of hiding places and recognise faces, and they learn from watching others.
explain: Scientists test bird minds with puzzles: a tube of water with a treat floating too low, a wire to bend, a box with several locks.
fact: Some crows in Japan drop nuts at pedestrian crossings for cars to crack and pick them up safely when the lights stop the traffic.
fact: New Caledonian crows make hooks from twigs to pull grubs out of holes, and a crow called Betty once bent a straight wire into a hook.
fact: In experiments, rooks and crows dropped stones into a tube of water to raise a floating treat, like the crow in Aesop's fable, and chose stones that sink.
fact: Crows recognise human faces; in Seattle, crows scolded a mask worn by people who had caught some of them for years and taught other crows to do it.
fact: Jays and nutcrackers hide thousands of nuts in autumn and remember most of the places months later, though the nuts they forget can grow into new trees, and jays re-hide food if another jay was watching.
fact: Alex, a grey parrot studied by Irene Pepperberg, learned over 100 words and could name colours, shapes and numbers up to six.
fact: Wild parrots copy the calls of their flock; a pet parrot treats its people as its flock, so it copies their words, polite or rude.
fact: Some parrots live for over 60 years, so a pet parrot may outlive its owner.
fact: Keas, mountain parrots of New Zealand, are very curious puzzle solvers, because food is scarce high in the mountains and exploring everything pays off, and are known for pulling rubber off parked cars.
fact: Some magpies tried to remove a mark they could only see in a mirror, which suggests they recognise themselves, though the mirror test is argued about.
fact: Ravens have been filmed sliding down snowy roofs again and again, apparently for fun, and they can save a tool to use later.
fact: Pigeons have learned to tell paintings by Monet from paintings by Picasso.
exercise: Why do some crows in Japan drop nuts at road crossings? | So cars crack them and the crows collect them when traffic stops | To feed the drivers | To bury them deep in the road where squirrels cannot dig them up | To hit people walking by
exercise: What did the crow Betty do with a straight wire? | She bent it into a hook to lift food | She used it as a toothpick after eating | She threw it hard at the other crows in the cage | She buried it like a nut
exercise: Why do New Caledonian crows make hooks? | To pull grubs out of holes in wood | To hang their nests up on high branches | To dig for water in the hard dry ground | To scratch their backs where they itch
exercise: How did crows reach a treat floating low in a tube of water? | They dropped in stones to raise the water | They drank all the water in the tube first | They tipped the whole tube over with their beaks | They waited for it to rain
exercise: Which stones did the crows choose for the water tube? | Heavy ones that sink | Light ones that float | Only white ones they could see | Any stone at all, big or small
exercise: Why might a crow follow one man across a park, shrieking? | It recognises his face and remembers he was a danger | It wants to sit on his head | Crows shriek at every single person who walks through a park | It has lost its way
exercise: How long did the Seattle crows remember the dangerous mask? | For years, and they taught other crows | For about a minute | Only until it got dark that night | They forgot it straight away, as soon as it was gone
exercise: What does a jay do with acorns in autumn? | It hides thousands and remembers most places months later | It eats them all in one day | It throws them at squirrels | It forgets them all, which is the only way oaks can ever grow
exercise: What does a jay do if it sees another jay watching it hide food? | It moves the food to a new hiding place later | It gives all of its food away to the other jay | It sings a loud warning song | It eats the other jay's food
exercise: What could Alex the grey parrot do? | Name colours and shapes and count up to six | Read whole books out loud to all his keepers | Speak five different languages with his keepers | Do long division sums in his head without help
exercise: Why does a pet parrot copy people's words? | It treats its people as its flock and copies their calls | It understands every sentence it says, just like a person | Parrots only copy rude words | It is trying to sing music
exercise: What is a kea known for? | Curiosity and puzzle solving, even pulling rubber off cars | Being the slowest and the sleepiest parrot in all the world | Living in deserts | Never coming near people
exercise: What does the mirror test check? | Whether an animal knows the reflection is itself | Whether it likes shiny things | How good its eyesight is | Whether it can count
exercise: What did ravens do on a snowy roof? | Slid down again and again, apparently for fun | Built nests in the snow | Ate the snow to stay cool | Hid from the cold under the snow until the sun came out
exercise: Why is «bird brain» a poor insult? | Many birds are very clever, with tightly packed brain cells | Birds have no brains at all | Birds have the very biggest brains of all the animals on Earth | Only parrots have brains
exercise: What could pigeons learn to do with paintings? | Tell Monet's paintings from Picasso's | Paint pictures just like Monet | Eat the paint | Hang them up straight on the walls
test: «don't be a bird brain, mate» Why might a scientist laugh at that? | Crows and parrots are among the cleverest animals | Birds have the biggest brains on Earth | Birds can talk like people | Only fish are clever
test: Aesop's fable says a thirsty crow dropped stones into a jug. When scientists tested it with real birds, what happened? | The birds dropped stones to raise the water, choosing sinking ones | The birds gave up at once | The birds broke the jug | The birds dropped only floating corks, because corks are lighter to carry
test: «that crow's been screaming at me since I poked its nest last year, can't be the same bird» What is most likely? | It is the same crow; crows remember faces for years | Crows forget everything overnight | All crows hate everyone | It is a different crow that just happens to be there by chance
test: What would happen if a jay forgot where it hid its nuts? | Some forgotten nuts would grow into trees | The nuts would roll back to the jay by themselves | The jay would never need food again that winter | The other jays would bring the nuts back to it
test: In a cartoon a parrot repeats every rude thing anyone says. What is true? | Parrots do copy sounds of their flock, and to a pet its people are the flock | Real parrots only say rude things | Parrots never copy any sound | Parrots understand every word they say and choose the rude ones on purpose to annoy
test: «got a parrot for my birthday, he'll be gone in a few years» What could be wrong with that? | Some parrots live over 60 years | Right, parrots live about a year | Parrots live only as long as goldfish | Parrots never grow old or get ill at all
test: A kea pulls the rubber strip off a parked car in the mountains. Why do keas test everything? | In the high mountains food is scarce, so exploring pays off | They are trying to fix the car | They are scared of cars | They eat rubber as their main food, because there is nothing else up there
test: A magpie with a sticker on its neck looks in a mirror and pecks at its own neck. What might this show? | It may know the reflection is itself | It thinks another magpie stole its sticker | It wants to eat the shiny mirror glass | It is cleaning the glass with its beak
test: «a raven slid down the roof like 6 times, is it broke or what» What is the best answer? | It is probably playing, as ravens are known to | It is ill and cannot fly, so it slides | It is trying to melt the snow with its warm feathers | It is falling asleep
test: Count them: 4 crows and 2 parrots sit on a fence. How many wings and how many feet? | 12 wings and 12 feet | 6 wings and 6 feet | 12 wings and 24 feet | 8 wings and 12 feet
test: A crow and a man see the same cracked nut on a crossing. The light turns red for cars. What will a clever crow do? | Hop down and take the nut while the cars are stopped | Fly at the cars | Wait until the cars start moving again and then fly in | Leave the nut for the man
exam: Identify the behaviour exhibited by the New Caledonian crow designated Betty in the course of experimental observation. | The bending of a straight wire into a hook for the retrieval of food | The recitation of words of human speech | The construction of a nest from wire supplied by the experimenters | The counting of objects up to six
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the recognition of human faces by crows. | Crows recall dangerous faces for years and communicate this to other crows | Crows are incapable of distinguishing one human face from another | Crows recall faces for no longer than a single day | Crows recognise only the faces of children
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the behaviour of jays in respect of stored food? | They recover most of their caches months later and re-hide food when observed | They store food only in their own nests | They are incapable of recalling any cache | They consume all food immediately upon finding it, storing none whatsoever for the winter
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a domesticated parrot imitates human speech. | It treats the members of the household as its flock | It comprehends the full meaning of every utterance | It is incapable of producing natural calls | It imitates only words of a discourteous character
